# ASYSMA 1-2-3-4 Design Record — 2026-08-25

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A[1 — Native Foundation] --> B[2 — Host and Integrity]
    B --> C[3 — Execution Bridge]
    C --> D[4 — Java / Application]
    A --> N[Native OS representation]
    N --> B
    B --> P{Entry Type}
    P -->|JAVA| J[SecureJDK 28]
    P -->|NATIVE| X[Native Application]
    P -->|NATIVE_THEN_JAVA| T[Native Bootstrap] --> J
    J --> APP[Java Application]
```

## 1 — Native Foundation

The initial ASYSMA execution model targets modern x86-64 Intel/AMD systems and relies on the operating system's native executable loader. ASYSMA does not replace ELF, PE/COFF, or Mach-O.

The native bootstrap is intentionally small. It establishes the platform handoff and provides the boundary into the ASYSMA runtime rather than attempting to become a second operating system.

## 2 — Host and Integrity

After native startup, ASYSMA establishes a host profile and evaluates package integrity before application execution.

Known observations include:

- CPU architecture
- operating-system family
- operating-system version
- available memory
- available storage
- required runtime capabilities

Integrity and policy remain separate concerns. A valid signature or hash does not by itself grant authorization.

Malformed offsets, sizes, architecture fields, or handoff structures must cause controlled refusal rather than execution.

## 3 — Execution Bridge

ASYSMA supports three explicit entry modes:

```text
JAVA
NATIVE
NATIVE_THEN_JAVA
```

### JAVA

```text
.asysma → integrity/policy → SecureJDK 28 → Java
```

### NATIVE

```text
.asysma → native bootstrap → Direct/host profile → policy → native application
```

### NATIVE_THEN_JAVA

```text
.asysma → native bootstrap → Direct/host profile → integrity/policy → SecureJDK 28 → Java
```

A native component is always declared explicitly. Java classes do not silently confer native execution capability.

## 4 — Java and Application Layer

The ordinary Java compilation contract remains:

```text
Java source → javac → .class
```

ASYSMA packaging is a subsequent step:

```text
.class + metadata + optional native payload → ASYSMA packager → .asysma
```

A future SecureJDK 28 toolchain may provide a convenient source-to-ASYSMA workflow without requiring `javac` itself to become a native compiler.

The reference managed runtime for the initial profile is SecureJDK 28.

## Current implementation concerns

1. Freeze the binary ASYSMA header encoding.
2. Define the concrete integrity/cryptographic profile.
3. Implement the ASYSMA reader and bounds checker.
4. Implement the packer for `.class` and native payloads.
5. Implement native-to-Java handoff.
6. Define the XMC compiler/packaging role if the repository's XMC component is introduced.
7. Preserve the CMD-origin icon identity for initial ASYSMA packages.
8. Keep format, Direct interface, native bootstrap, Java bridge, and SecureJDK versions independently negotiable.

## Status

This dated record captures the design state for 2026-08-25. It is a design/engineering record, not a claim that every binary runtime component listed above has already been implemented.
